---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const mdaCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Mechanics', note: 'The rules and systems: jump height, resource costs, cooldowns. The only part the designer writes directly.' },
    { math: '2 · Dynamics', note: 'What the mechanics do when played: emergent chases, economies, betrayals. The designer shapes these indirectly.' },
    { math: '3 · Aesthetics', note: 'What the player feels: tension, mastery, camaraderie. The actual goal of the design — everything else is means.' },
    { math: '4 · Work backwards: A → D → M', note: 'Design by naming the feeling first, imagining the dynamics that create it, and only then choosing mechanics. MDA read backwards is a design method.' },
    { math: '5 · Prototype, then argue with the playtest', note: 'Paper prototypes expose the loop in minutes. Players will use your mechanics in ways you did not plan — that discovery is the work.' },
  ],
};

const loopCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '100*(1 - exp(-0.7*x))', label: 'player mastery (a.u.)', color: '#0d9488', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '12*x', label: 'challenge level', color: '#dc2626', width: 2.0 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'player experience (hours)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 130, label: 'a.u.' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 330,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/game-development/foundational/game-design-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Game design is the craft of engineering emotion through rules. A game is a system the player
    learns, exploits and eventually masters — and the designer never touches the player directly,
    only the rules. The discipline's shared framework, MDA (Mechanics–Dynamics–Aesthetics),
    formalises that distance: write mechanics, watch them become dynamics, hope for aesthetics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the MDA framework, read both directions" widget="equationStep" config={mdaCfg} />

  <h2>The core loop and the difficulty curve</h2>
  <p>
    Good games run on a loop — act, receive feedback, grow, face a harder test. The designer's
    hardest dial is the gap between rising challenge and rising skill: too far apart either way and
    play collapses into anxiety or boredom. Flow lives where the two curves stay close.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="mastery rises, challenge must follow — flow is the gap" widget="plotter" config={loopCfg} />

  <h2>Decisions are the atoms of play</h2>
  <p>
    Strip any good game to its skeleton and you find interesting decisions: choices with
    trade-offs, under uncertainty, with readable consequences. Rock-paper-scissors has one; chess
    has a lifetime's worth. Rules that produce no decisions produce no game — however beautiful
    they look.
  </p>

  <h2>Prototype before you polish</h2>
  <p>
    The industry's hard-won rule is to test the loop as cheaply as possible: cards and counters
    before code, grey boxes before art. Mechanics that feel flat on paper rarely bloom in 3D, and
    ones that surprise on paper deserve the studio's months. Everything else in game development —
    engines, graphics, audio — exists to serve the loop you verified here.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hunicke, LeBlanc &amp; Zubek, "MDA: A Formal Approach to Game Design" (2004); IGDA Curriculum Framework; Schell, <em>The Art of Game Design</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
